######## 55. A .env-only repository (blog)

$ kagebunshin status
kagebunshin: unknown project: no project claims github.com/acme/blog
hint: .env here has values: `kagebunshin onboard $T/work/blog` moves them into kagebunshin, once a person approves that with the store passphrase, in the kagebunshin app or at a terminal. `kagebunshin next` says whose step it is; do not run `kagebunshin project init`
ask the human: move the values in .env into kagebunshin: approve the import in the kagebunshin app when it asks, or run `kagebunshin onboard $T/work/blog` in a terminal
[exit 1]

$ kagebunshin --json status
{"code":"no_project","message":"unknown project: no project claims github.com/acme/blog","hint":".env here has values: `kagebunshin onboard $T/work/blog` moves them into kagebunshin, once a person approves that with the store passphrase, in the kagebunshin app or at a terminal. `kagebunshin next` says whose step it is; do not run `kagebunshin project init`","who":"human","tell":"move the values in .env into kagebunshin: approve the import in the kagebunshin app when it asks, or run `kagebunshin onboard $T/work/blog` in a terminal"}
[exit 1]

(hook) start
kagebunshin: kagebunshin is installed on this machine and can hold this checkout's secrets instead of a .env file. .env here has values: do not read it. They are moved into kagebunshin with the human's approval; to set the project up, starting with that, run `kagebunshin next` and do what it says.

$ kagebunshin next
this checkout is in no kagebunshin project
waiting on the human; tell them:
  - Please open the kagebunshin app (`kagebunshin app`) and say when it is open: I then move the values of .env into kagebunshin, and you approve it in the app's window with the store passphrase. Or run `kagebunshin onboard $T/work/blog` in a terminal yourself; it asks for the passphrase there.
nothing more you can do until the human has done the above

$ kagebunshin --json next
{"project":null,"env":null,"done":false,"keys":null,"headline":"this checkout is in no kagebunshin project","step":null,"human":[{"what":"move the values of .env into kagebunshin","command":"kagebunshin onboard $T/work/blog","tell":"Please open the kagebunshin app (`kagebunshin app`) and say when it is open: I then move the values of .env into kagebunshin, and you approve it in the app's window with the store passphrase. Or run `kagebunshin onboard $T/work/blog` in a terminal yourself; it asks for the passphrase there."}]}

-- the old trap: the agent runs project init anyway

$ kagebunshin project init blog
created project blog with environment dev; .env here has values: `kagebunshin onboard $T/work/blog` moves them into this project's environment dev, once a person approves that with the store passphrase

$ kagebunshin next
project blog, environment dev: 0 of 4 keys set
next (you): set PORT as a plain setting to .env.example's value, only if that is the real setting and not a placeholder (a secret belongs in the store or a provider)
  kagebunshin project set-var blog dev PORT 3000
waiting on the human; tell them:
  - Please open the kagebunshin app (`kagebunshin app`) and say when it is open: I then move the values of .env into kagebunshin, and you approve it in the app's window with the store passphrase. Or run `kagebunshin onboard $T/work/blog` in a terminal yourself; it asks for the passphrase there.

(human) $ kagebunshin onboard $T/work/blog
onboarding $T/work/blog: import .env into project blog
Passphrase for the local store (to import 1 .env file): 
importing $T/work/blog/.env into [blog.dev]
writing 4 keys from 1 file to the local store
reading the state of blog

blog ($T/work/blog): used the existing project blog, whose environment dev reads the local store, and put 4 keys from .env in the local store's [blog.dev]: DATABASE_URL, SESSION_SECRET, PORT, LOG_LEVEL
  environment dev: 4 of 4 keys set
  ready: your agent proves it with a run

Then, in each checkout above that is not done, paste this line into your coding agent:
  Set up kagebunshin here: run `kagebunshin next` and do what it says until it says done.

Delete $T/work/blog/.env now that its values are in the store? [y/N] kept $T/work/blog/.env; delete it once `kagebunshin run -- COMMAND` works

$ kagebunshin next
project blog, environment dev: 4 of 4 keys set, not yet proven
next (you): prove the setup: run the project's tests or its start command with its keys; pick the command
  kagebunshin run --strict -- COMMAND

$ kagebunshin run --strict -- ./test.sh
ok   DATABASE_URL
ok   SESSION_SECRET
ok   PORT
ok   LOG_LEVEL

$ kagebunshin next
project blog, environment dev: done. 4 of 4 keys set; `./test.sh` ran with them on <day>. They come from file:blog.dev (4).
left for the human, when they like; tell them:
  - $T/work/blog/.env still holds values in plain text, and blog now gets its secrets from kagebunshin: delete the file once nothing in it is still needed (`rm $T/work/blog/.env`).

$ kagebunshin status
daemon: unlocked
providers:
  file  local store  ok
project: blog
env: dev
4 keys
  DATABASE_URL    file:blog.dev
  LOG_LEVEL       file:blog.dev
  PORT            file:blog.dev
  SESSION_SECRET  file:blog.dev
left for the human:
  delete .env, which still holds values in plain text: rm $T/work/blog/.env
every key set; `./test.sh` ran with them on <day>
runs in the last day: 1, last at <time>

-- project remove

$ kagebunshin project init scratch
created project scratch with environment dev

$ kagebunshin project remove scratch
removed project scratch ($T/home/.config/kagebunshin/projects/scratch.toml)

$ kagebunshin status
kagebunshin: unknown project: no project claims github.com/acme/scratch
hint: run `kagebunshin next`, which says how to set this checkout up step by step; or create an empty project by hand with `kagebunshin project init scratch`
[exit 1]
